            <ArticleTitle>Social and psychosocial influences on inflammatory markers and vascular function in civil servants (the Whitehall II study).</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>984-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Social position and psychosocial factors are associated with coronary disease, but the underlying pathophysiologic mechanisms remain unclear. In a sample of 283 nonsmokers, we found that social position was inversely associated with interleukin-6 and C-reactive protein and that participants with mild depression had impaired endothelial function.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
